Package: faciolock
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         faciolock-models (= ${source:Version})
Recommends: libpam-faciolock (= ${binary:Version})
Description: IR face authentication for Linux - daemon and CLI
 Faciolock authenticates a user from the near-infrared camera that ships on
 laptops with Windows Hello hardware. It is designed as a platform component:
 a socket-activated system daemon that holds the templates and the camera, and
 a PAM module that owns no secrets and only asks a question.
 .
 This package contains faciolockd, the faciolockctl enrolment and diagnostic
 CLI, the systemd units and the polkit policy. The recommended
 libpam-faciolock package enables face authentication by default.
 .
 Face authentication is a convenience factor, not a password replacement. A
 password always remains reachable. See /usr/share/doc/faciolock/README.Debian.

Package: libpam-faciolock
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         libpam-runtime (>= 1.0.1-6),
         faciolock (= ${binary:Version})
Description: IR face authentication for Linux - PAM module
 The PAM module half of Faciolock. It contains no cryptography, no camera code
 and no template data; it connects to the daemon's socket, asks whether the
 named user is present, and returns. Its entire dependency tree is libc and the
 project's own IPC crate, because it is loaded into processes that are root
 while they run.
 .
 The module registers a pam-auth-update profile enabled by default, with
 password fallback. faciolockctl can turn the profile on or off. Recovery
 instructions are in /usr/share/doc/libpam-faciolock/README.Debian.

Package: plasma-faciolock-kcm
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         faciolock (= ${binary:Version}),
         libpam-faciolock (= ${binary:Version}),
         libkf6kcmutils-bin,
         systemsettings,
         qml6-module-qtquick,
         qml6-module-qtquick-controls,
         qml6-module-qtquick-layouts,
         qml6-module-org-kde-kirigami,
         qml6-module-org-kde-kcmutils
Recommends: plasma-faciolock-greeters (= ${binary:Version})
Description: IR face authentication for Linux - KDE Plasma settings module
 A System Settings page for Faciolock, and the same page as a "Face Unlock"
 entry in the application launcher. It shows whether you are enrolled and
 whether face authentication is switched on, and it enrols, tests and removes
 your own face.
 .
 It holds no privilege of its own. Every action runs faciolockctl through
 pkexec against the polkit action the daemon package already ships, so the
 panel can do exactly what you could do from a terminal and nothing more. It
 can switch face authentication on or off through faciolockctl, which applies
 the shared PAM profile and keeps the normal password path available.
 .
 Two kinds of dependency here are not found by dh_shlibdeps and are listed by
 hand. The five qml6-module-* packages are what the panel imports at runtime;
 those imports live inside a Qt resource in the plugin, where no ELF scan can
 see them, and without them the page loads and renders nothing. The two
 programs are kcmshell6 for the launcher entry and systemsettings for the
 System Settings entry.

Package: plasma-faciolock-greeters
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         libpam-faciolock (= ${source:Version}),
         plasma-desktop-data,
         sddm,
         sddm-theme-breeze,
         qml6-module-qtcore,
         qml6-module-org-kde-kirigami
Description: Automatic face scanning in Plasma and SDDM greeters
 A Breeze based SDDM theme and a Plasma lock screen wrapper that start a face
 authentication attempt when the greeter appears. An animated face indicator
 appears during the attempt, and the normal password controls remain available.

Package: faciolock-models
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: IR face authentication for Linux - ONNX models
 The neural network weights Faciolock runs: YuNet for face detection and
 landmark extraction, SFace for the 128-dimension embedding. Both come from the
 OpenCV Zoo and carry their upstream licences, which are vendored alongside
 them.
 .
 They are packaged separately so that a security update to the daemon does not
 re-ship 38 MB of weights, so that the licence accounting stays legible, and so
 that a different model set can be substituted. The daemon verifies every file
 against a SHA256 manifest before loading it.
